" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87c025ce-7c7a-5364-86fe-6c2155437fe3" :question/problem "![](images/44/44c489cbdf4665cca7fc7b8a331f5ccf4d72f8d15577f58c45497c58d5817bf3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c4cf3906e5af81f6cb071bae5b594861351987934fe6d51443c09e17982b2204.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=12 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 12 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b5e35f-78cc-50f2-aaa7-c2505886c068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}-1\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519773} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0a32b4-436c-52b0-8fcd-a18894aa9935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519780} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdc303e-a1be-599a-9867-656643cf200d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519801} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ad259-18a1-56c3-a654-80f395f4a9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}7 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519820} {:answer/id #uuid "dc61cd39-2fb7-5f0f-8d2b-5a86a92eda96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdc303e-a1be-599a-9867-656643cf200d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519801} :answer-field/id #uuid "8686d10f-d81d-5379-a992-d03cbd3fde80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519810}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9515597d-52f8-5675-a35e-118e53d6193a" :question/problem "![](images/44/44c489cbdf4665cca7fc7b8a331f5ccf4d72f8d15577f58c45497c58d5817bf3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=4$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=3$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c4cf3906e5af81f6cb071bae5b594861351987934fe6d51443c09e17982b2204.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3389ec8a-5ead-590b-9ac7-88add8627653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r \\left(3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519784} {:answer/id #uuid "495eed6a-8a13-519f-a611-b69ba073a16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519791} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed0bc57-bfca-5e00-ba52-1de4c390c35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519794} {:answer/id #uuid "55140ddf-99ac-5982-98f8-93d907c32218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}- r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519797} {:answer/id #uuid "ba902bbe-b7e8-556a-ade5-171834fc2fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495eed6a-8a13-519f-a611-b69ba073a16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519791} :answer-field/id #uuid "061e0e7c-46a8-5dad-ab08-61f43bd85732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519775}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e01ec71-3cc7-57cc-814c-d392156f806f" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1dc30e767ab459dd4625582c1d06a14f506a8600b3b47ff0cf650e160d5f9d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/88/888f97120e097c46a7dfdcfb137fbae11b71e1606ade407f7c644468de4ede2c.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 6 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=6 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 6 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 6 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2511112b-841c-51d6-8d4d-b3539a193a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519782} {:answer/id #uuid "527a7dca-4a36-570b-a68e-a02e1073c9c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519795} {:answer/id #uuid "669ada49-68c4-5dcc-b8ee-035124baf168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519805} {:answer/id #uuid "7b47de44-c51e-5c10-8ad0-40e138ed665b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}6 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519806} {:answer/id #uuid "c8807c08-a1d5-52a7-854d-06af06353c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}-2\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669ada49-68c4-5dcc-b8ee-035124baf168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519805} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4892de7-98ec-52b0-b09f-85ff129235db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcd4852-bd78-56bb-b0b5-886d7bd31914" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=4$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1120e481-e454-5ed8-9848-d43606c711d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}- 3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519778} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba9dbe8-0bd6-5c6f-805b-f866cec39b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519813} {:answer/id #uuid "95e5225f-bd3e-5c05-b72d-3618f5471707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519815} {:answer/id #uuid "c21e252e-e313-5930-bdac-6fd13a6cad09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}5 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519824} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb546e9-faf7-52eb-b1ea-ecf0a1ef22cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95e5225f-bd3e-5c05-b72d-3618f5471707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519815} :answer-field/id #uuid "62acbc22-b618-5647-b810-b46d46c380b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e830914b-72a8-5550-9a2a-83a9bba0628e" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c7fc0c4d92c68dad3d8607f25f7be80d79ca7b2d412b67b0942a669725e3d36.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=4$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4b3e6446a0ae02a08e91d527f402287151ddb3f39c7b744503430cb9697e03a.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(4-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 5,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(3\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}]}
